findings.body:
The setter check was loosened from `value <= 0` to `value < 0`:

::diff{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/AzureFileLoggerOptions.cs" lines="48-59"}

`FileLoggerProvider.RollFiles()` only deletes files when `_maxRetainedFiles > 0`:

::code{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/FileLoggerProvider.cs" lines="74-88"}

If someone sets `RetainedFileCountLimit = 0` (perhaps expecting "keep none"), the value now passes validation and the provider never deletes anything. Log files pile up without limit and can fill an App Service's disk quota.

::callout{type="warning"}
The check now contradicts the docs ("strictly positive value") and its own exception message ("must be positive"). A docs-only PR shouldn't change it.

::suggestion{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/AzureFileLoggerOptions.cs" lines="53-53"}
            if (value <= 0)

The setter check was tightened from `value < 0` to `value <= 0`:

::diff{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/BatchingLoggerOptions.cs" lines="42-53"}

This public options type has shipped (see `PublicAPI.Shipped.txt`). Before this change, `0` was accepted when the options were bound. It only failed later in `BatchingLoggerProvider.Start()`, and only when the logger was enabled. Configs that set `BackgroundQueueSize: 0` with the logger disabled now throw during options binding.

The exception text also still says `"must be non-negative"`, and `0` *is* non-negative. So the message now contradicts the check, which makes the error confusing to diagnose.

This behavior change has no place in a docs PR. Revert it. If rejecting 0 is actually wanted, do it in a separate PR with a breaking-change note and a fixed message.

::suggestion{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/BatchingLoggerOptions.cs" lines="47-47"}
            if (value < 0)

`TryGetValidatableTypeInfo` now sets `validatableTypeInfo = null` *before* the loop instead of after it:

::diff{file="src/Validation/src/ValidationOptions.cs" lines="44-56"}

Each resolver call overwrites the out variable, so the early assignment does nothing. Once the trailing reset is gone, a resolver that returns `false` but leaves a non-null value in the out parameter has that value leak out with a `false` result. That breaks the documented "otherwise, null" contract.

It also makes the method differ from `TryGetValidatableParameterInfo` just below, which still resets after the loop. That method also gained a `{` on the signature line, which goes against the repo's Allman brace style:

::code{file="src/Validation/src/ValidationOptions.cs" lines="66-77"}

Revert both edits. This PR is supposed to change docs only.

::suggestion{file="src/Validation/src/ValidationOptions.cs" lines="44-56"}
